 11	 *
 12	 * A player builds the plant before the mine that would run the planet short, not after the mines have
 13	 * already stalled: the host multiplies a planet's whole production by the ratio of the energy it can
 14	 * cover, so a planet in deficit mines at a fraction of what its mines say and everything it builds
 15	 * afterwards is worth less. That is why the canonical opening in every guide starts with the solar
 16	 * plant rather than with a mine, and why this asks about the *next* level instead of about the balance
 17	 * as it stands.
 18	 *
 19	 * Which buildings can answer it is the host's decision, not a list of names: the candidates are the
 20	 * objects the host itself reports as producing, filtered to those the building queue accepts whose next
 21	 * level puts energy out. Whatever an extension adds to that set is offered here the moment the host
 22	 * knows about it. The cheapest capacity is tried first, which is why a player builds a solar plant long
 23	 * before a fusion reactor.
 24	 *
 25	 * Only capacity is produced here. When the account cannot pay for it yet the plan falls through to
 26	 * whatever else the host accepts, and the account keeps playing -- which is what a player does while
 27	 * saving.
 28	 */
